<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gutasaga</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Guta saga</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gutasaga is a short Old Gutnish account of the early history of Gotland, written down in the thirteenth century and preserved with the island's law, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gutalag">Gutalag</a>. It tells of the legendary settler Tjelvar, who brought fire to the island, of the emigration of part of its people, and of the treaty by which Gotland paid a yearly tribute to the Swedish king in return for protection.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gutasaga.jpg" alt="Gutasaga" width="320" height="340" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Berig · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Snake-witch.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The saga survives in a single <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, Codex Holm. B 64 of about 1350, kept at the National Library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>. In its story Gotland lay under a spell, under water by day and rising only at night, until Þieluar broke it by lighting a fire. His son Hafþi married Hwitastierna, White-star, who dreamed of three snakes entwined in her bosom; Hafþi read the dream as a prophecy of three sons and named them in an old alliterative verse, probably from oral tradition: Guti, Graipr and Gunfiaun. The brothers divided the island into northern, middle and southern thirds, though prose and verse disagree over which was eldest.</p><p>When their descendants grew too many for the land, every third person was chosen by lot to leave, taking everything they owned except their land. The emigrants went to Þorsburg, then to Faroy, then to Dagaiþi, an island close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, and up the Dyna through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> until they came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, where, the writer says, they still lived and still kept something of the islanders' language. The saga also names Awair Strabain as the man who arranged the agreement with the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, and sets out the duties of the Swedish king and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> towards Gotland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The emigration story is associated with the migration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, whose first appearance in classical sources fits it: Eusebius of Caesarea reported that they devastated Macedonia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, the Pontus and Asia in 263. The mention of the Dvina may reflect the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a>, when the Dvina-Dniepr waterway replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> as the main trade route to Greece for the Gutes. Because it presents Gotland's ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> as resting on mutual agreement, the saga is read as an assertion of the island's independence as well as a history.</p><p>The threefold division it describes remained in law until 1747 and survives in the church's three deaneries. A stone ship at Boge is called Tjelvar's Grave, and in 2011 an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> asteroid was named 137052 Tjelvar after the first man to bring fire to the island.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gutasaga” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Gutasaga</b> records an agreement under which the islanders paid a yearly tribute to the king of the Svear in return for protection.</blockquote><a href="/state/gotland">Gotland</a></li></ul></section>
